PENDING PATCH — "a DEMOTE states its own scope" (intended v0.31.8)
VERSION COLLISION CORRECTED 2026-09-13: this was written as "intended v0.31.7", but
v0.31.7 SHIPPED on 2026-09-13 carrying the correctness queue (items 0-7) and NOT this
patch. The filename is historical; the target is the NEXT version. A parked patch
claiming a version that already exists is how a future session applies it twice or
believes it is already live.
Written 2026-09-12. NOT APPLIED to the working tree. NOT SYNTAX-CHECKED, NOT SUITE-TESTED.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  The sandbox mount was down for the whole 2026-09-11 session, so this change was
  written with file tools and could not be run through `node --check` or
  `node test-delivery.js`. It was applied to src/sources/calendar/cadence-watch.js
  and test-delivery.js, then DELIBERATELY REVERTED, because:
    - market-radar.vbs is in Windows Startup, so an unattended reboot (a Windows
      update overnight) would have launched the bot from the working tree;
    - a parse error throws at import time, BEFORE any boot gate runs, so the
      self-tests offer no protection — they never execute;
    - the result would be a 15-second crash-loop with no alerts, during the week
      MOVE's and ORDER's windows close;
    - the daily backup covers data/, not source, and these edits existed in exactly
      one place, uncommitted.
  Reverting restored a known-good live path. This file is the work, kept whole, so
  nothing had to be chosen between "risk the bot" and "lose the change".

HOW TO REAPPLY (shell required)
  1. node test-delivery.js            <- must be ALL GREEN on the CURRENT tree first
  2. apply PATCH 1, 2 and 3 below, verbatim
  3. node --check src/sources/calendar/cadence-watch.js && node --check test-delivery.js
  4. node test-delivery.js            <- section 55 must appear and pass; section 30
                                         (prose lint) has NEVER seen this wording and
                                         is the check most likely to object
  5. only then: version bump, FORCE-RESTART, verify the banner, PUSH-TO-GITHUB.bat

WHAT IT DOES
  ENA demoted 2026-09-10 on a SINGLE-WALLET spec while carrying two unwatched
  co-emitters. Single-wallet was the deliberate choice (an irregular emitter would
  false-demote every quiet month) and the opposite failure then occurred: the verdict
  cannot tell a stopped schedule from a migration to another wallet. This is the
  claim-coverage rule applied to the verdict instead of the alert — it does NOT fold
  the co-emitters into the band, which would restore the failure single-wallet was
  chosen to prevent. It makes the verdict state what it read and what it therefore
  cannot distinguish.

